ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)
==================================================
Ham (July 1957 – January 19, 1983), a  [LINK: (chimpanzee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimpanzee]  also known as Ham the Chimp and Ham the Astrochimp, was the first  [LINK: (great ape) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape]  launched into  [LINK: (space) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outer_space] . On January 31, 1961, Ham flew a  [LINK: (suborbital flight) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suborbital_flight]  on the  [LINK: (Mercury-Redstone 2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury-Redstone_2]  mission, part of the U.S. space program's  [LINK: (Project Mercury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Mercury] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-csrvlmt-1]  [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-chsent-2]

Ham was known as "No. 65" before he safely returned to Earth, when he was named after an acronym for the laboratory that prepared him for his historic mission—the Holloman Aerospace Medical Center, located at  [LINK: (Holloman Air Force Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holloman_Air_Force_Base]  in New Mexico, southwest of  [LINK: (Alamogordo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alamogordo,_New_Mexico] . His name was also in honor of the commander of Holloman Aeromedical Laboratory, Lieutenant Colonel Hamilton "Ham" Blackshear. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Swenson-3]  [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Brown-4]


# EARLY LIFE #

Ham was born in July 1957 in  [LINK: (French Cameroon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Cameroon] , [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-NASA_Brief_History-5]  [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Nicholls-6]  captured by animal trappers and sent to the  [LINK: (Rare Bird Farm) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami_Rare_Bird_Farm]  in  [LINK: (Miami, Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami,_Florida] . He was purchased by the  [LINK: (United States Air Force) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Air_Force]  and brought to Holloman Air Force Base in July 1959. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-NASA_Brief_History-5]  Ham was sold to the United States Air Force for $457. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Nicholls2011-7]

There were originally 40 chimpanzee flight candidates at Holloman. After evaluation, the number of candidates was reduced to 18, then to six, including Ham. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Animals_in_Space-8] : 245–246  Officially, Ham was known as No. 65 before his flight, [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Smithsonian-9]  and only renamed "Ham" upon his successful return to Earth. This was reportedly because officials did not want the bad press that would come from the death of a "named" chimpanzee if the mission were a failure. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Haraway-10]   Among his handlers, No. 65 had been known as "Chop Chop Chang". [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Patch-11]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Haraway-10]


# TRAINING AND MISSION #

Beginning in July 1959, the two-year-old chimpanzee was trained under the direction of neuroscientist  [LINK: (Joseph V. Brady) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_V._Brady]  at  [LINK: (Holloman Air Force Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holloman_Air_Force_Base]  Aero-Medical Field Laboratory to do simple, timed tasks in response to electric lights and sounds. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-12]  During his pre-flight training, Ham was taught to push a lever within five seconds of seeing a flashing blue light; failure to do so resulted in an application of a light  [LINK: (electric) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity]  shock to the soles of his feet, while a correct response earned him a banana pellet. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-This_New_Ocean-13] : 312  Ham was trained for 219 hours during a 15-month period. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Rohles1963-14] : 21

While Ham was the first great ape, he was not the first animal to go to space; there were many other types of  [LINK: (animals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animals_in_space] , including  [LINK: (primates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primates] , that had left Earth's atmosphere before him. However, none of these other animals could provide the significant insight that Ham could provide. One of the reasons that a  [LINK: (chimpanzee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimpanzee]  was chosen for this mission was because of their many similarities to humans. Some of their similarities include: similar organ placement inside the body and having a response time to a stimulus that was very similar to that of humans (just a couple of deciseconds slower). Through the observations of Ham scientists would gain a better understanding of the possibility of sending humans into space. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Smithsonian-9]

On January 31, 1961, Ham was secured in a  [LINK: (Project Mercury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Mercury]  mission designated  [LINK: (MR-2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury-Redstone_2]  and launched from  [LINK: (Cape Canaveral, Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Canaveral_Air_Force_Station] , on a suborbital flight. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-csrvlmt-1]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-This_New_Ocean-13] : 314–315  Based on dental eruption, Ham was 44 months old at the time of the flight. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Rohles1963-14] : 21

A number of physiological sensors were used to monitor the vital signs ( [LINK: (electrocardiogram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrocardiogram] , respiration, and body temperature) of Ham. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Ward1963-15] : 25  A commercial rectal thermistor probe was used instead of the probe used on the human Mercury astronauts. [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Wheelwright1962-16]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Ward1963-15] : 27  The probe was inserted 8 inches deep into Ham's rectum. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Ward1963-15] : 27  The physiological sensors were placed on Ham about 10 hours before liftoff. [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Stingely1963-17] : 9  Ham's ability to complete tasks during the flight were assessed by the psychomotor apparatus. [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Brown1963-18] : 15  The apparatus gave Ham a visual cue in the form of colored lights and required a response from two levers; if he succeed in his task, drink and food pellet would be awarded; failure would be punished by a shock to the soles of his feet. [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Brown1963-18] : 15–16

Due to a valve malfunction, the Redstone rocket delivered thrust higher than intended. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]  The anomaly triggered the emergency escape rocket and subjected Ham to 17  [LINK: (g) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G-force]  of acceleration. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]  The jettison of the spent escape rocket also caused the retro rocket pack to be prematurely jettisoned. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]  The lack of the retro rocket caused the capsule to reenter the atmosphere with excessive speed. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]  Ham was subjected to 14.7 g during reentry. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]  Ham's capsule splashed down in the  [LINK: (Atlantic Ocean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Ocean]  and was recovered by the  [LINK: (USS Donner) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Donner_(LSD-20)]  later that day. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-This_New_Ocean-13] : 316  The capsule was damaged during splashdown and settled deeper in the water than designed. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Burgess2014-19]

The post flight examination found a small abrasion on the bridge of Ham's nose; he was also dehydrated and lost 5.37% body weight; he was otherwise in good physical condition. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Ward1963-15] : 29  His flight was 16 minutes and 39 seconds long. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-NASA_stats-20]  He would become agitated when the press approached him and panic when his handler would try to situate him into a capsule for photos. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Alexander1966-21] : 316, 576

Ham's lever-pushing performance in space was only a fraction of a second slower than on Earth, demonstrating that tasks could be performed in space. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-This_New_Ocean-13] : 316  Of the two shocks Ham received in flight, the one shortly after the launch was due to an error in the testing apparatus; the other one due to the lack of response after experiencing 14 g deceleration during reentry. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Rohles1963-14] : 22–23  The results from his test flight led directly to  [LINK: (Alan Shepard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Shepard] 's May 5, 1961, suborbital flight aboard  [LINK: (Freedom 7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury-Redstone_3] . [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-22]


# LATER LIFE #

Ham retired from the  [LINK: (National Aeronautics and Space Administration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA]  (NASA) in 1963. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-:0-23]  On April 5, 1963, Ham was transferred to the  [LINK: (National Zoo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Zoological_Park_(United_States)]  in Washington, D.C., where he lived for 17 years [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Animals_in_Space-8] : 255–257  before joining a small group of chimps at  [LINK: (North Carolina Zoo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Carolina_Zoo]  in  [LINK: (Asheboro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asheboro] , on September 25, 1980. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-24]

Suffering from chronic heart and liver disease, Ham died on January 19, 1983, at age 25. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-:0-23]  [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-25]  Following a  [LINK: (necropsy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropsy]  by the  [LINK: (Armed Forces Institute of Pathology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armed_Forces_Institute_of_Pathology] , Ham's body was to be  [LINK: (taxidermied) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxidermied]  and placed on display at the  [LINK: (Smithsonian Institution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithsonian_Institution] , following Soviet precedent with pioneering  [LINK: (space dogs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_space_dogs]   [LINK: (Belka and Strelka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belka_and_Strelka] . However, after negative public reaction, the plan was abandoned. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-26]  Ham's skeleton is held in the collection of the  [LINK: (National Museum of Health and Medicine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Museum_of_Health_and_Medicine] , Silver Spring, Maryland, [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Nicholls-6]  and the rest of his remains were buried at the  [LINK: (International Space Hall of Fame) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Mexico_Museum_of_Space_History#International_Space_Hall_of_Fame]  in Alamogordo, New Mexico.  [LINK: (Colonel John Stapp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Stapp]  gave the eulogy at the memorial service. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-27]

Minnie, Ham's backup, was the only female chimpanzee trained for the Mercury program. After her role in the Mercury program ended, Minnie became part of an Air Force chimpanzee breeding program, producing nine offspring and helping to raise the offspring of several other members of the chimpanzee colony. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Animals_in_Space-8] : 258–259  Minnie was the last surviving astro-chimpanzee and died on March 14, 1998 at the age of 41. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-Animals_in_Space-8] : 259


# CULTURAL REFERENCES #

[LINK: (Tom Wolfe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Wolfe] 's 1979 book  [LINK: (The Right Stuff) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Right_Stuff_(book)]  depicts Ham's spaceflight, [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-28]  as do its  [LINK: (1983 film) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Right_Stuff_(film)]  and  [LINK: (2020 TV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Right_Stuff_(TV_series)]  adaptations.

The 2001 film  [LINK: (Race to Space) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Race_to_Space]  is a fictionalized version of Ham's story; the chimpanzee in the film is named "Mac". [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-29]

In 2007, a French documentary made in association with  [LINK: (Animal Planet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_Planet] , Ham—Astrochimp #65, tells the story of Ham as witnessed by Jeff, who took care of Ham until his departure from the Air Force base after the success of the mission. It is also known as Ham: A Chimp into Space / Ham, un chimpanzé dans l'espace. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-30]

The 2008 3D animated film  [LINK: (Space Chimps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Chimps]  follows anthropomorphic chimpanzees and their adventures in space. The primary protagonist is named Ham III, depicted as the grandson of Ham. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-31]

In 2008,  [LINK: (Bark Hide and Horn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bark_Hide_and_Horn] , a folk-rock band from Portland, Oregon, released a song titled "Ham the Astrochimp", detailing the journey of Ham from his perspective. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_(chimpanzee)#cite_note-32]


